Track both of a contact's security slots in ims_usrloc_pcscf.

A pcontact has two security slots, security and security_temp, with the
unprefixed and the t_* columns behind them, for the handover TS 33.203 7.4
describes: the SA set a 401 offers is only a candidate until a REGISTER over it
is answered 200, and until then the one the UE registered over keeps carrying
everything. Only security_temp has ever been used, and the code that looks at a
contact's tunnel looks there alone:

- get_all_ucontacts() lists one record per IPsec slot, so
  delete_unused_tunnels() leaves the tunnel in either alone.
- unreg_pending_contacts_cb() also matches a pending contact whose offer is
  the registered contact's security, so that contact's expiry does not destroy
  a tunnel promoted to security. A pending contact holds only the offer
  save_pending() stored, so only its security_temp is compared.
- db_update_pcontact_security() no longer writes the IPsec protocol into
  "protocol". Unlike t_protocol, that column is not part of the security set
  but the contact's transport, written by db_insert_pcontact(): writing "esp"
  over it corrupts the contact.

Nothing changes until something fills security. With ims_ipsec_pcscf as it is
it stays NULL, and each of these reads exactly what it did before. Whatever
starts filling it also has to tear it down on expiry and own the previous
value, which update_security() does not free: patches 0013 and 0014.

